14. Interview Explanation

Apache Spark follows a master-worker architecture. The Driver acts as the brain of the application. It contains the SparkSession, SparkContext, DAG Scheduler, and Task Scheduler. When a Spark application starts, the Driver analyzes the code, builds a DAG of transformations, divides it into stages and tasks, and requests resources from the Cluster Manager. The Cluster Manager allocates executors on worker nodes. Executors are JVM processes responsible for executing tasks in parallel, caching data in memory, performing shuffle operations, and returning results to the Driver. The Driver monitors execution and combines the final output. This distributed architecture allows Spark to process massive datasets efficiently using parallel execution and in-memory computation.

15. Key Interview Points to Remember

Spark uses a Master–Worker Architecture.
The Driver is the brain of the Spark application.
SparkSession is the entry point for DataFrame and SQL APIs.
SparkContext is the low-level connection to the Spark cluster.
The DAG Scheduler creates stages from transformations.
The Task Scheduler creates and assigns tasks to executors.
The Cluster Manager allocates cluster resources but does not execute data processing.
Worker Nodes are physical or virtual machines that host executors.
Executors are JVM processes that execute tasks, cache data, and return results.
A Job is created only when an Action (such as show(), collect(), count(), or write()) is called.
Spark achieves high performance through parallel processing, in-memory computation, and fault tolerance.
